          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "In medical coding, a combination code is a single code that represents two conditions, or a condition and a complication. When a combination code exists for a particular set of circumstances, it is used instead of coding each condition separately. This is why some disease families, such as diabetes, appear to have many codes (from “ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection”).\n\nBased on the provided references, conditions that are coded using a combination code include:\n\n*   **Diabetes** and conditions related to it, such as diabetic chronic kidney disease and a diabetic foot ulcer (from “ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection”, “ICD-10-CM and WHO ICD-10: why a code found in one may not exist in the other”, and “Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories”).\n*   **Asthma** (from “ICD-10-CM and WHO ICD-10: why a code found in one may not exist in the other”).\n*   **Hypertensive heart disease** (from “Coding heart failure: I50, its mechanism axis and its acuity axis” and “Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family”).\n*   **Hypertension with chronic kidney disease** (from “Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family”).\n*   **Secondary hypertension**, which is coded together with its underlying cause (from “Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family”).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
